**switch-case Statement:**

**Syntax:**
```java
switch (expression) {
    case value1:
        // Code for value1
        break;
    case value2:
        // Code for value2
        break;
    case value3:
        // Code for value3
        break;
    default:
        // Code if no case matches
}
```

**Complete Example:**
```java
int day = 3;

switch (day) {
    case 1:
        System.out.println("Monday");
        break;
    case 2:
        System.out.println("Tuesday");
        break;
    case 3:
        System.out.println("Wednesday");  // This executes
        break;
    case 4:
        System.out.println("Thursday");
        break;
    case 5:
        System.out.println("Friday");
        break;
    case 6:
        System.out.println("Saturday");
        break;
    case 7:
        System.out.println("Sunday");
        break;
    default:
        System.out.println("Invalid day");
}
```

**Fall-Through Behavior:**

**Without break - Falls through to next case:**
```java
int number = 2;

switch (number) {
    case 1:
        System.out.println("One");
        // No break - falls through
    case 2:
        System.out.println("Two");    // Executes
        // No break - falls through
    case 3:
        System.out.println("Three");  // Also executes!
        break;
}
// Output: Two Three
```

**Intentional Fall-Through (Grouping Cases):**
```java
char grade = 'B';

switch (grade) {
    case 'A':
    case 'B':
    case 'C':
        System.out.println("Passed");  // All three grades execute this
        break;
    case 'D':
    case 'F':
        System.out.println("Failed");
        break;
    default:
        System.out.println("Invalid grade");
}
```

**String switch (Java 7+):**
```java
String month = "January";

switch (month) {
    case "January":
    case "February":
    case "December":
        System.out.println("Winter");
        break;
    case "March":
    case "April":
    case "May":
        System.out.println("Spring");
        break;
    default:
        System.out.println("Other season");
}
```

**Supported Types:**
- ✅ int, byte, short, char
- ✅ String (Java 7+)
- ✅ Enums
- ❌ long, double, float, boolean

**switch vs if-else:**

**Use switch When:**
- Checking exact values (equality)
- Multiple fixed values
- Checking same variable
- Cleaner for 3+ comparisons

```java
// switch - Better for fixed values
switch (day) {
    case 1: System.out.println("Monday"); break;
    case 2: System.out.println("Tuesday"); break;
    case 3: System.out.println("Wednesday"); break;
}
```

**Use if-else When:**
- Range checks
- Complex conditions
- Boolean expressions
- Different variables
- Relational operators (>, <, >=, <=)

```java
// if-else - Better for ranges
if (score >= 90) {
    System.out.println("A");
} else if (score >= 80) {
    System.out.println("B");
}

// if-else - Better for boolean
if (age >= 18 && hasLicense) {
    System.out.println("Can drive");
}
```

**Common Mistakes:**

```java
// ❌ MISTAKE 1: Forgetting break
switch (x) {
    case 1:
        System.out.println("One");
        // Missing break - falls through!
    case 2:
        System.out.println("Two");
}

// ❌ MISTAKE 2: Missing default
switch (month) {
    case 1: System.out.println("Jan"); break;
    // No default - silently fails for invalid input
}

// ❌ MISTAKE 3: Using unsupported types
switch (price) {  // ERROR! double not supported
    case 19.99: ...
}
```

**Best Practices:**
- Always include break (unless intentional fall-through)
- Always include default case
- Comment intentional fall-through
- Use switch for readability with multiple fixed values

code:
public class SwitchDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int day = 3;

        switch (day) {
            case 1:
                System.out.println("Monday");
                break;
            case 2:
                System.out.println("Tuesday");
                break;
            case 3:
                System.out.println("Wednesday");
                break;
            default:
                System.out.println("Other day");
        }

        // Grouping cases
        char grade = 'B';
        switch (grade) {
            case 'A':
            case 'B':
            case 'C':
                System.out.println("Passed");
                break;
            case 'D':
            case 'F':
                System.out.println("Failed");
                break;
            default:
                System.out.println("Invalid");
        }
    }
}

