**Ternary Operator - Compact if-else:**

**Syntax:**
```java
variable = (condition) ? valueIfTrue : valueIfFalse;
```

**Basic Examples:**
```java
// Simple example
int age = 20;
String status = (age >= 18) ? "Adult" : "Minor";
System.out.println(status);  // "Adult"

// Even/Odd check
int number = 7;
String result = (number % 2 == 0) ? "Even" : "Odd";
System.out.println(result);  // "Odd"

// Maximum of two numbers
int a = 10, b = 20;
int max = (a > b) ? a : b;
System.out.println(max);  // 20

// Minimum
int min = (a < b) ? a : b;
System.out.println(min);  // 10
```

**Equivalent if-else:**
```java
// Ternary
String status = (age >= 18) ? "Adult" : "Minor";

// Equivalent if-else
String status;
if (age >= 18) {
    status = "Adult";
} else {
    status = "Minor";
}
```

**When to Use Ternary:**

**✅ Good Uses:**

**1. Simple assignments:**
```java
int discount = (isMember) ? 20 : 0;
String grade = (score >= 60) ? "Pass" : "Fail";
int abs = (num >= 0) ? num : -num;
```

**2. Method arguments:**
```java
System.out.println((count == 1) ? "1 item" : count + " items");
setColor((isActive) ? "green" : "gray");
```

**3. Return statements:**
```java
public String getStatus(int age) {
    return (age >= 18) ? "Adult" : "Minor";
}
```

**❌ Bad Uses (Use if-else Instead):**

**1. Nested ternary (hard to read):**
```java
// BAD - Nested ternary
String grade = (score >= 90) ? "A" :
               (score >= 80) ? "B" :
               (score >= 70) ? "C" : "F";

// GOOD - Use if-else
String grade;
if (score >= 90) grade = "A";
else if (score >= 80) grade = "B";
else if (score >= 70) grade = "C";
else grade = "F";
```

**2. Complex conditions:**
```java
// BAD - Complex condition
String status = (user != null && user.isActive() &&
                user.hasPermission("admin")) ?
                "Authorized" : "Unauthorized";

// GOOD - Use if-else for clarity
String status;
if (user != null && user.isActive() && user.hasPermission("admin")) {
    status = "Authorized";
} else {
    status = "Unauthorized";
}
```

**3. Side effects (confusing):**
```java
// BAD - Side effects in ternary
int value = flag ? count++ : count--;

// GOOD - Use if-else
if (flag) {
    count++;
} else {
    count--;
}
int value = count;
```

**4. Multiple statements:**
```java
// IMPOSSIBLE - Cannot use multiple statements
// (condition) ? (action1; action2;) : (action3; action4;);

// Use if-else
if (condition) {
    action1();
    action2();
} else {
    action3();
    action4();
}
```

**Comparison Table:**

| Aspect | Ternary | if-else |
|--------|---------|--------|
| Lines of code | 1 line | 3-5 lines |
| Readability | Simple only | Any complexity |
| Multiple statements | ❌ No | ✅ Yes |
| Debugging | Harder | Easier |
| Assignment only | ✅ Best | Also works |
| Complex logic | ❌ Avoid | ✅ Best |

**Best Practices:**
```java
// ✅ DO: Simple, clear assignments
int max = (a > b) ? a : b;
String msg = (count == 0) ? "Empty" : "Has items";

// ❌ DON'T: Nest ternary operators
int x = (a > b) ? (c > d ? 1 : 2) : 3;  // Confusing!

// ❌ DON'T: Complex conditions
int y = (a > b && c < d && e == f) ? 1 : 0;  // Hard to read

// ✅ DO: Use for simple boolean to int/string
int sign = (num >= 0) ? 1 : -1;
String parity = (num % 2 == 0) ? "even" : "odd";
```

**Rule of Thumb:**
If you need to think twice about the ternary, use if-else instead!

code:
public class TernaryDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Simple ternary
        int age = 20;
        String status = (age >= 18) ? "Adult" : "Minor";
        System.out.println(status);  // Adult

        // Max of two numbers
        int a = 10, b = 20;
        int max = (a > b) ? a : b;
        System.out.println("Max: " + max);  // 20

        // Even/Odd
        int num = 7;
        String parity = (num % 2 == 0) ? "Even" : "Odd";
        System.out.println(parity);  // Odd

        // AVOID nested ternary
        int score = 85;
        // BAD:
        String grade = (score >= 90) ? "A" :
                       (score >= 80) ? "B" : "C";

        // BETTER: Use if-else for multiple conditions
    }
}

